use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/FormParticipation import FormParticipation

export ui-component PinInput:
    description:
        A pin input is a row of small boxes for typing a short code of fixed
        length, such as a one-time code sent by text message. Each box holds
        one character, and focus moves along the row as the user types.

    behaviors:
        - @{FormParticipation}

    value:
        The characters in the boxes, read together as one piece of text. It
        takes part in a form as that one piece, not as one value per box.

    length:
        The component sets how many boxes there are, usually four or six. The
        number doesn't change while the user is typing.

    characters:
        By default only digits are accepted. The component can allow letters,
        or letters and digits, or give its own rule. A character that doesn't
        fit is not entered, and the component is told.

    typing:
        Typing a character fills the focused box and moves focus to the next
        one. Typing into a box that is already filled replaces what's there.
        Focus doesn't skip ahead to later empty boxes on its own.

    deleting:
        Backspace clears the focused box, or if it is already empty, moves back
        to the one before and clears that. Delete clears the focused box
        without moving.

    keyboard:
        Left and Right move between the boxes. For right-to-left languages the
        boxes run right to left. Whether each box is its own stop in the Tab
        order is up to the chosen library.

    pasting:
        Pasting a whole code fills every box from the first, wherever focus
        was. Spaces and dashes are removed first, so `123-456` fills six
        boxes. The component can mark it as a one-time code, so the device can
        offer to fill in a code it has just received.

    complete:
        The component is told when every box is filled, whether by typing or
        pasting. It can then choose to submit the form straight away, or to
        move focus out of the last box.

    masking:
        The component can hide the characters as they are typed, the way a
        password is hidden.

    name:
        The whole row has a name, such as "Verification code". Each box also
        has a name that says where it is, such as "Digit 2 of 6".

    disabled:
        A disabled pin input can't be focused or changed. A read-only one
        shows its characters but they can't be changed.

    nonGoals:
        - Not for codes of unknown or varying length, or for passwords. Those
            are TextFields
        - It doesn't decide whether the code is right. The component checks it
            and shows any error
